  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/.... \nThe weather pattern for most of next week will see the \nsubtropical high becoming the dominate feature across the \nSouthwestern U.S. while its strength will barely change. Guidance \nagrees the ridge center will shift from Texas on Monday to \nsomewhere over Arizona by Wednesday with H5 heights rising to \n595-597dm. H5 heights of this magnitude will represent the 97th \npercentile of climatology for the period and temperatures will \nfollow with forecast highs only falling short of daily records by \na few degrees each day. Since moisture levels are now expected to\nremain fairly limited for much of, if not all of, next week, the \nday-to-day convective potential should remain quite low and \nlikely confined to far eastern Arizona. The most likely scenario \nkeeps the ridge in full control over the Desert Southwest with the\ncenter hovering somewhere over Arizona and/or into western New \nMexico.\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence for a more prolonged heat \nevent leading to the now Extreme Heat Warning covering all lower\ndeserts and extending through Friday. NBM forecast highs show \nreadings anywhere from 108-113 degrees across the lower deserts \neach day next week with overnight lows near the 90 degree mark by\nTuesday-Wednesday as surface dew points shift a bit higher. \nForecast Major HeatRisk has also expanded more across the western \nlower deserts going into the middle part of next week with the \nbulk of the area keeping Major HeatRisk levels through at least \nnext Friday. There is uncertainty in the length of time this heat \nevent will last as some of the guidance shows the ridge center may\neventually shift more to the north and/or east. If this occurs, \nit should help to increase the amount of monsoon activity over the\nregion, eventually leading to some degree of cooling.",
